as you know earth is divided into time  zones but when did the idea of the time  zone come about and when was the current  day formalized system of time zones  created so first of all people have  known for a very long time that the  world is round and thus that it was day  time on different parts of earth at  different times the thing is for the  overwhelming majority of human history  it was completely irrelevant because  when you were traveling by foot by ship  or by horse who would move too slowly to  notice the difference so when did time  zones become a broader concept that  mattered to the average person well it  happened around the mid 19th century and  was down to two major technological  advancements the first being the  railways because it was considered  somewhat important not to have two  trains on the same track at the same  time and the second was the Telegraph so  the first country to introduce a time  zone was the United Kingdom this  happened in 1847 when railway operators  switched from the numerous local times  which were often several minutes off of  each other to Greenwich Mean Time  this was regulated by the Greenwich  Observatory who had a permanent  Telegraph link to the railway stations  across the country would update them  with accurate times to set the clocks to  now just because the railways adopted  these measures didn't mean the rest of  the country did and the process of  creating a unified time for the nation  took many decades and in fact the  primary pusher for this wasn't business  but workers unions and their  parliamentary representatives there was  a fairly common practice a factory  owners buying clocks that intentionally  ran slow during the day so that the  workers would work ten and a half hours  whilst only believing they work ten and  another reason was the desire to control  when alcohol could be sold in order to  legislate for both of these time had to  be standardised a process completed with  the 1880 definition of time act which  split the United Kingdom into two time  zones Greenwich Mean Time and Dublin  Mean Time which was 25 minutes behind  the same process happened shortly  afterwards in the United States which  itself adopted what's called Standard  Time in 1883 this was the result of  discussions between American and  Canadian railroad tycoons who had  collectively decided to split  continental North America into five time  zones later reduced to four these time  zones were wildly different to the ones  used today and also the American  government and public weren't consulted  in the end there was a much the US  government could do about it and so in  1918 it made it official and passed it  into law so what about globally well  after the  adopted standard time in 1883 president  Chester Arthur called for a convention  to standardize time across international  borders they saw delegations from the  world's great powers and also Liberia  who discussed which country would be  home to the universal time and the  country selected was the United Kingdom  much to the annoyance of the French  delegation who refused to accept this  change and continue to use their own  parish time until 1911 when they  reluctantly signed up to the  international standard and thus the  international system of standardized  time zones was created and over the next  century the world's nations signed up to  it the last one being Liberia despite  having agreed to it nearly a century  prior I hope you enjoyed this episode  and a special thanks to all my patreon  supporters James vis annette marvin  castle a man of culture kelly moneymaker  danny Meloni Jon biscuit Rob Waterhouse  Moe Aaron the white James Castaneda  Gustav Swan Jordan long ray  Marcus Ozma spinning three plates  cooling Castleman filled oink oink  Rashid Ali David Silverman  easy Maggie packs Kowski Spencer  Lightfoot of Winston K word Robert  Wetzel Lexy Schwinn Anthony Beckett and  Skye Chappell 90 years Liberia get it  together
 

